    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d351">

                <pb facs="0368" n="356" sameAs="frus:frus1939v01/pg356"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">760C.62/951: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Italy</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Phillips</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Rome</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-08-23T22:00:00+01:00">August 23, 1939—10
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received August 23—5:10 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>325. Immediately upon receipt of the President’s message I called upon Count
                        Ciano<anchor n="40" xml:id="d351-f1"/> and requested an audience with the
                    King who is now near Turin in northern Italy. Ciano consulted with Mussolini
                    over the telephone and subsequently arranged an audience for 2:00 p.m. tomorrow.
                    Consequently I am leaving this evening on the 11:00 o’clock train for Turin.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Phillips</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d351-f1" n="40" xml:id="d351-f1-content">
                <p>Galeazzo Ciano di Cortellazza, Italian Minister for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
